  <section class="fv-section">
    <div class="container">

    <p>From neurology to pain medicine, the goal was never "inject wherever it hurts." Colan Clinic exists for people who have tried many treatments and still haven't found the real answer.</p>

    <h2>Why Luzhou</h2>
    <p>Dr. Lin grew up in the Sanluzh area and noticed a gap: despite being part of New Taipei City, this district has had very little access to precise pain diagnostics.</p>
    <p>The community here is a mix of retirees, office workers, and an increasingly active sports population — pickleball courts and soccer fields are popping up everywhere, and sports injuries are on the rise. Yet most local options still stop at traditional rehabilitation and orthopedic clinics.</p>
    <p>Neurology-trained pain clinics are rare in this area. A practice that integrates neurology, pain management, and regenerative medicine is essentially absent. Being close to home also makes it easier to coordinate specialty referrals when needed.</p>
    <p>This isn't about chasing patient volume. It's about filling a gap that genuinely exists here.</p>

    <h2>A Neurologist's View of Pain</h2>
    <p>Most pain clinics come from orthopedics, anesthesiology, or rehabilitation — focused on structural stability of muscles, bones, and joints. That matters, but it's only part of the picture.</p>
    <p>At its core, pain requires a nerve signal reaching the brain. Many patients have normal imaging — ultrasound, MRI, X-ray all clear — yet the pain persists. This is extremely common in clinical practice.</p>
    <p>Some examples: post-stroke patients develop strange, treatment-resistant pain months later; post-herpetic neuralgia leaves the nerve sheath damaged and hypersensitive; diabetic peripheral neuropathy causes chronic pain and numbness that many hospitals simply label "untreatable."</p>
    <p>This is where neurology training has a genuine edge. Combined with peripheral nerve intervention, a 60–80% improvement in this type of pain is achievable for many patients.</p>

    <h2>Ultrasound Is a Diagnostic Tool, Not Just a Targeting Aid</h2>
    <p>Many people assume ultrasound-guided care means "locate and inject." That's only half of it.</p>
    <p>The more important role of ultrasound is dynamic examination. Unlike X-ray or MRI — where the patient must lie still — ultrasound lets us observe in real time while the patient moves. We can watch whether a joint catches, whether a ligament shifts, whether a nerve glides freely through its surrounding tissue at the exact angle that causes pain.</p>
    <p>Some fascial or intramuscular changes don't show on MRI but are visible under dynamic ultrasound. Pressing with the transducer at the suspected site while the patient reproduces their movement can confirm a diagnosis that no static image would reveal.</p>

    <h2>What Is Nerve Dynamic Assessment</h2>
    <p>Watching nerve mobility under ultrasound is one core component. In carpal tunnel syndrome, for example, we ask the patient to flex the wrist and move the fingers while watching in real time whether the median nerve glides smoothly or is tethered to surrounding tissue. Shoulder impingement, trigger finger mechanics, and rotational pain patterns can all be assessed the same way.</p>
    <p>Nerve conduction and electromyography are complementary static tools — they assess the "wiring" itself: whether the signal travels normally, whether the insulation is intact, whether there's a break somewhere upstream.</p>
    <p>Each method has strengths and limits, with accuracy around 70–80% separately. Combined, they reach over 90%. After an injection, nerve dynamic assessment can confirm immediately whether adhesion has improved — rather than waiting three to six months for nerve conduction studies to catch up.</p>

    <h2>Normal MRI, Still in Pain — Why</h2>
    <p>This is one of the most common situations in clinic. Patients arrive with a normal MRI report and no idea where to go next.</p>
    <p>MRI slice thickness is typically 2 to 5 mm. It detects water content and magnetic field changes, which means it has a sensitivity ceiling — it cannot catch everything. Its strength is in deep pathology and signal differences; it's more consistently accurate than ultrasound for certain conditions. But it's done lying still, and symptoms that only appear under load or in a specific posture won't show up.</p>
    <p>A normal MRI can mean: no spinal compression (which is useful to rule out), a problem that only appears under dynamic load (requiring dynamic ultrasound), or a structurally intact system where the nerve itself has become sensitized or central pain processing has shifted. Each scenario needs a different approach — one imaging study is rarely the whole answer.</p>

    <h2>Blocking Pain vs. Repairing It</h2>
    <p>Corticosteroids block inflammation. For a genuine acute flare — red, hot, swollen, within two weeks — they work well and can last a long time. But for chronic degeneration, repeated ligament strain, or long-standing nerve adhesion, steroids only suppress symptoms temporarily. They come back, and repeated injections can weaken tissue further.</p>
    <p>Regenerative treatment addresses the tissue itself. Ligament fiber tears, localized cartilage wear, perineural scarring — these need glucose, PRP, amniotic membrane, or bone marrow aspirate to create the conditions for actual repair.</p>
    <p>The prerequisite for regenerative treatment is a clear diagnosis. Injecting regenerative material without knowing exactly what and where rarely gives either party the result they hoped for.</p>

    <h2>Who Is a Good Fit for Evaluation</h2>
    <p>Several situations are particularly well-suited for a full assessment here.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Rehabilitation for three or more months with improvement that plateaued halfway — progress stopped and won't restart. This usually means there's a diagnostic layer still unaddressed.</li>
      <li>PRP done elsewhere with no result, where the preparation process or injection site may be the issue. Not all PRP is equivalent; re-evaluation often finds a correctable variable.</li>
      <li>Multiple treatments, symptoms keep recurring, no clear cause found. This is the most common presentation here — and the situation this clinic is built for.</li>
      <li>Acute injury where you want to protect the window for healing and avoid long-term sequelae. The period around one week post-injury is often the best time to intervene and give tissue the best chance at recovering close to its original state.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Who May Not Be the Right Fit</h2>
    <p>Some situations are beyond what this clinic can realistically address.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Severe spinal compression with lower limb weakness or bladder and bowel involvement — these require surgical evaluation, not injection therapy.</li>
      <li>Expecting one session to resolve everything permanently with no follow-up maintenance. Even elite professional athletes with access to the best available treatment don't return to a completely symptom-free baseline.</li>
      <li>Unwilling to accept any injection or invasive treatment, and unwilling to add training — this combination rarely produces meaningful improvement in chronic pain.</li>
      <li>Unable to attend follow-up or unwilling to complete a full evaluation process. A proper regenerative assessment requires several data points over time; without that, the process doesn't work as intended.</li>
    </ul>
